(window.LADDER_CARDS["∞Ψ"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["∞Ψ"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"∞Ψ.1.1","q":"What does the fixed-point technique say, and what is read down the diagonal? Why does a fixed-point-free f from B to B rule out a namer?","a":"If φ : A × A → B names every function from A to B, then every f from B to B has a fixed point; read down the diagonal is g(x) = f(φ(x, x)), which has a name a, so b = φ(a, a) has f(b) = b; turned about, an f that keeps nothing fixed leaves no such b, so no φ names every function","w":["If φ names every function then no f has a fixed point; the diagonal is g(x) = φ(x, x) alone; a fixed-point-free f proves a namer exists","Every function from A to B has a fixed point; the diagonal is the list of names; a fixed-point-free f shows A is empty","If some f has a fixed point then φ names every function; the diagonal is f(φ(a, b)) over all pairs; a fixed-point-free f is no function"],"why":"g must be named first, g = φ(a, −); only then is φ(a, a) read."},
 {"u":"∞Ψ.2.1","q":"How is 35c1’s parts-outrun technique a case of the fixed-point technique, and what are A, B and f? Why is the part {x : x ∉ F(x)} the function g of the proof?","a":"A is the set, B = {1, 0}, keep and drop, and f = not, which keeps nothing fixed; a function from A to B is a part of A, so an F from A onto the parts would give φ(x, y) = 1 exactly when y ∈ F(x), naming every function, which the technique forbids; g(x) = not φ(x, x) keeps x exactly when x ∉ F(x), so g is that part","w":["A is the parts, B is the set and f does nothing; the part is g because F picks it by hand","A = B = {1, 0} and f = not; the part {x : x ∉ F(x)} is the image of F, so F reaches it","A is the set, B = {1, 0} and f swaps nothing; g is the part of members in their own image, x ∈ F(x)"],"why":"Rung 35 found g by hand; the frame says it is f applied down the diagonal."},
 {"u":"∞Ψ.3.1","q":"How is 2e14’s outsider set a case of the fixed-point technique? Why does the sift axiom stop φ from naming every function?","a":"Under the old rules a set U of all sets would hold every part of itself as a member, so with A = U, B = {1, 0} and φ(x, y) = 1 exactly when y ∈ x, φ names every function; g(y) = not φ(y, y) is the outsider set, and its name r gives r ∈ r exactly when r ∉ r, so there is no such U; the sift axiom only sifts a set already in hand, so a condition alone gives no set and φ does not name every function","w":["The outsider set is the fixed point b itself; the sift axiom stops φ by forbidding not","With A = U and B = U, f is membership; the sift axiom makes every condition a set, so φ names more functions","The outsider set shows that not has a fixed point; the sift axiom takes the diagonal out of U"],"why":"2e14’s outsider set is g, and r is its name."},
 {"u":"∞Ψ.4.1","q":"How is 38h2’s self-statement technique the positive form of the fixed-point technique? Why must the substitution be a recipe that always stops?","a":"A is the formulas with one placeholder, B the statements of arithmetic, and φ(D, E) puts the code of D into E; every f(χ) = ψ(⌜χ⌝) then has its fixed point δ = D₀(⌜D₀⌝), with PA proving δ exactly when ψ(⌜δ⌝), so here the frame builds; the diagonal g must be named by a formula D₀, and that needs sub to be a recipe that always stops","w":["It shows that no ψ has a fixed point, as not has none; the substitution must stop so that PA is complete","A is the statements and B the formulas, and φ lists proofs; the substitution must stop so that δ is false","It takes f = not and finds no fixed point; the substitution need not stop, since only its code is used"],"why":"Here no f fails to have a fixed point: every ψ gets its δ."},
 {"u":"∞Ψ.5.1","q":"How are 38k2 and 41d2 cases of the fixed-point technique, and what plays not in each? Why would truth be a fixed point of not if a truth formula existed?","a":"In 38k2, f is not on statements: with ψ = not True, 38h2 gives δ true exactly when not True(⌜δ⌝), while True says δ is true exactly when True(⌜δ⌝), so truth would be fixed by not; in 41d2, A is the codes of machines, B = {stops, runs on} and “the opposite” plays not: a stop decider would make the flipped diagonal a machine E, named by its own code ⟨E⟩","w":["In both, f does nothing and the fixed point is harmless; truth would be fixed because every statement is true or false","In 38k2 not is played by True, in 41d2 by the stop decider; truth would be a fixed point because δ has no code","38k2 uses the positive form and builds a truth formula; 41d2 builds a stop decider from the diagonal"],"why":"The same four lines, with a different A."},
 {"u":"∞Ψ.6.1","q":"Why do programs not name every function from ℕ to ℕ? What does a fixed-point-free f rule out, and what does it leave alone?","a":"There are as many programs as whole numbers and more functions (35c1), so programs name only what a machine computes; a fixed-point-free f rules out the namer of every function, and it leaves f alone: not is still a function","w":["Because programs may run for ever, they name no function at all; a fixed-point-free f rules out f","Programs do name every function, so adding one has a fixed point; a fixed-point-free f rules out the set B","Because ℕ has no diagonal; a fixed-point-free f rules out the diagonal and leaves the namer alone"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["∞ל‎"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["∞ל‎"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"∞ל‎.1.1","q":"What does the one-right-side technique say? Why does an order need no check of naturality when one map is left of another?","a":"A map between orders has at most one right side: if f ⊣ g and f ⊣ g′ then g = g′, and the same for left sides with the order turned round; in an order there is at most one arrow between two things, so f ⊣ g says only that f(x) ≤ y exactly when x ≤ g(y), and naturality comes free","w":["Every map between orders has exactly one right side and one left side; naturality is free because orders have no arrows","A map between orders may have many right sides, all matched; naturality must still be checked square by square","If f ⊣ g then g ⊣ f; naturality is free because f and g are undoable"],"why":"So once pulling back is fixed, every and some are found, not chosen."},
 {"u":"∞ל‎.2.1","q":"What does the pull-back-keeps technique say about ∪ and about running g after f? Why do only four parts of X come back in the example, when Y has eight parts?","a":"f⁻¹(V ∪ W) = f⁻¹(V) ∪ f⁻¹(W), and (g ∘ f)⁻¹ = f⁻¹ ∘ g⁻¹: pull back along g, then along f; in the example nothing lands on c, so c adds nothing and the eight parts of Y come back as only ∅, {1, 2}, {3, 4} and X","w":["f⁻¹(V ∪ W) = f⁻¹(V) ∩ f⁻¹(W), and (g ∘ f)⁻¹ = g⁻¹ ∘ f⁻¹; four come back because f is one-to-one","f⁻¹ keeps ∪ only when f is a bijection, and g after f cannot be pulled back; four come back because Y has three members","f⁻¹(V ∪ W) = f⁻¹(V) ∪ f⁻¹(W), and (g ∘ f)⁻¹ = g⁻¹ ∘ f⁻¹; four come back because X has four members"],"why":"Membership is checked through f, so the order of running is turned round."},
 {"u":"∞ל‎.3.1","q":"What does the image-left technique say, and which word of rung 2 sits inside the image? Why is f[P ∪ Q] = f[P] ∪ f[Q] free once the image is a left side?","a":"f[P] ⊆ V exactly when P ⊆ f⁻¹(V), so taking images is left of pulling back; the word is some: y is in f[P] exactly when some x with f(x) = y is in P; a left side keeps sums by 47f4, and in an order of parts a sum is a union","w":["f⁻¹(V) ⊆ P exactly when V ⊆ f[P]; the word is every; the union is kept because f is one-to-one","f[P] ⊆ V exactly when P ⊆ f⁻¹(V); the word is every; a left side keeps products, and a product of parts is a union","f[P] = V exactly when P = f⁻¹(V); the word is not; the union is kept only after checking each member"],"why":"The image is the least V whose pull-back holds P."},
 {"u":"∞ל‎.4.1","q":"What does the every-image technique say? Why is c in the every-image of {1, 2, 3} but not in its image?","a":"f⁻¹(V) ⊆ P exactly when V ⊆ f∀[P], so the every-image is right of pulling back, and the only right side; over c sits nothing, and every member of nothing is in P, an empty truth, so c is in f∀[P] = {a, c}; but no x in P lands on c, so c is not in f[P] = {a, b}","w":["f[P] ⊆ V exactly when P ⊆ f∀[V]; c is in the every-image because 3 lands on c","f⁻¹(V) ⊆ P exactly when V ⊆ f∀[P]; c is in the every-image because some x over c is in P","V ⊆ f⁻¹(P) exactly when f∀[V] ⊆ P; c is in neither, since nothing lands on c"],"why":"f∀[P] = Y ∖ f[X ∖ P]: 2b15’s “not some not”, said of parts."},
 {"u":"∞ל‎.5.1","q":"What part of X × Y does a condition ψ(y) that does not mention x pull back to? Why is the fresh-name rule of 2b8 the same as the condition ψ living on Y?","a":"ψ is a part V of Y, and it pulls back along the reading π(x, y) = y to π⁻¹(V), the same condition with x added and not used; using a some is π[P] ⊆ V from P ⊆ π⁻¹(V): from φ(b, y) reach ψ(y), and ψ may not mention b exactly because ψ is a part of Y, where there is no x","w":["It pulls back to the image π[P]; the fresh-name rule says ψ must mention b, since ψ lives on X × Y","It pulls back to all of X × Y; the fresh-name rule is the every-image technique with V = π∀[P]","It pulls back to the part {y : ∃x φ(x, y)}; the fresh-name rule says b must be a member of Y"],"why":"The quantifiers are the two sides of pulling back along a reading."},
 {"u":"∞ל‎.6.1","q":"What does the sift-pulls-back technique say? Why is {n : n² is even} the pull-back of the evens along n ↦ n²?","a":"A sift by a condition read through f is the pull-back along f of the sift by the condition: {x ∈ X : ψ(f(x))} = f⁻¹({y : ψ(y)}); with f(n) = n² and ψ(y) “y is even”, n is in the sift exactly when f(n) is in the evens, which is what the pull-back of the evens says","w":["A sift is the image of its condition: {x : ψ(f(x))} = f[{y : ψ(y)}]; the evens are the image of the squares","Every sift is a pull-back along the do-nothing map only; n² is even because n ↦ n² is one-to-one","{x : ψ(f(x))} = f⁻¹({y : ψ(y)}) holds only for a bijection f; n ↦ n² is a bijection of the whole numbers"],"why":"Putting f(x) in for y is pulling back along f."},
 {"u":"∞ל‎.7.1","q":"What does the reach-modal technique say? Why does □P hold at w in the example while ◇P does not?","a":"With ◇P = s[t⁻¹(P)] and □P = s∀[t⁻¹(P)], □P = W ∖ ◇(W ∖ P); at w nothing can be reached, so every way reached from w is in P by an empty truth and □P holds, while ◇P needs some reached way in P and there is none","w":["□P = ◇P whenever R is a relation; at w both hold, since w reaches itself","□P = W ∖ ◇P; □P holds at w because w is in P, and ◇P fails because v is not","With ◇P = s∀[t⁻¹(P)] and □P = s[t⁻¹(P)], ◇P = W ∖ □P; □P holds at w because (u, w) is in R"],"why":"In the example □P = {v, w} and ◇P = {u, v}."},
 {"u":"∞ל‎.8.1","q":"Why does the image not keep meets, and what does a left side keep in their place? Why do P = {1} and Q = {2} refute f[P ∩ Q] = f[P] ∩ f[Q]?","a":"The image is a left side, and a left side keeps sums, here unions (47f4), not meets; P ∩ Q = ∅, so f[P ∩ Q] = ∅, while f[P] ∩ f[Q] = {a} ∩ {a} = {a}, since f(1) = f(2) = a","w":["The image is a right side and keeps meets only; P and Q refute it because f[P] ∩ f[Q] is empty","The image keeps neither unions nor meets; P and Q refute it because 1 and 2 land on different members","The image keeps meets for every f; P = {1} and Q = {2} refute only the every-image rule"],"why":"The image keeps meets when f is one-to-one, but only then."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["∞1"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["∞1"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"∞1.1.1","q":"What does the state-wrap technique say, and which technique of 47f2 does it use? Why does a function into T(B) wait for a starting state?","a":"Functions from A × S to B × S match functions from A to the wrapped type T(B) = the functions from S to B × S, by 47f2’s pair-to-function technique: fix the first input; a function into T(B) gives, for each a, a function of the state, so it gives no answer until a starting state s is put in","w":["Functions from A × S to B × S match functions from A × B to S, by 47f2’s pair technique; a function into T(B) waits because S is empty","Functions from A to B match functions from S to S, by fixing the output; a function into T(B) needs no state, since the state is hidden in the machine","Functions from A × S to B × S match functions from S to A × B, by 47f2’s pair-to-function technique; a function into T(B) waits for a second value of A"],"why":"The state is no longer hidden in the machine; it is in the type of the answer."},
 {"u":"∞1.2.1","q":"What does the maybe-row technique say, and why must the row open the wrapper? What are wrap and the row for the list wrapper?","a":"For the maybe wrapper, g ⋆ f fails at a exactly when f fails at a, or f succeeds with b and g fails at b; g wants a B and f gives a T(B), so the row must open the wrapper first; for lists, wrap makes the list of one, and the row runs g on each member of f(a) and joins the lists","w":["g ⋆ f fails only when both f and g fail; the row need not open the wrapper, since T(B) is B; for lists, wrap is the empty list and the row takes the first member","g ⋆ f never fails once f succeeds; the row opens the wrapper to throw the failure away; for lists, wrap repeats the value and the row sorts the lists","g ⋆ f fails exactly when g fails at a; the row opens the wrapper of g, not of f; for lists, wrap is the list of one and the row keeps only the longest list"],"why":"The row stops at the first failure; otherwise it passes b along."},
 {"u":"∞1.3.1","q":"What does the wrapped-category technique say, and which rule is the do-nothing demand? Why do both bracketings list the same h(c) in the same order?","a":"For a well-made wrapper, the types with the wrapped programs A → T(B) as arrows, η as do-nothing and ⋆ as running in a row, make a category; the do-nothing demand is g ⋆ η = g and η ⋆ f = f; both bracketings list h(c) for c in g(b), b in f(a), in that order, because joining lists does not care about brackets","w":["The wrapped programs make a group, with η as the undoing; the do-nothing demand is (h ⋆ g) ⋆ f = h ⋆ (g ⋆ f); the bracketings agree because lists have no order","The types with plain functions make a category whatever the wrapper is; the do-nothing demand is η ⋆ η = η; the bracketings agree only for lists of one","For a well-made wrapper every wrapped program can be undone; the do-nothing demand is g ⋆ f = f ⋆ g; the bracketings list the same h(c) in two different orders"],"why":"These are 47a1’s demands on a category."},
 {"u":"∞1.4.1","q":"What does the free-wrapper technique say, and what are η and the join for lists? Why is the list wrapper freeing after forgetting?","a":"For a left-and-right pair F ⊣ U, the wrapper U ∘ F has η the arrow A → U(F(A)) matched with the do-nothing of F(A), and the row joins with the product of F; for lists, η(x) = [x] and the join makes a list of lists into one list; the free joining on S is the finite lists of letters of S, and forgetting the joining keeps the set of lists, which is the list wrapper","w":["For any map F, the wrapper F ∘ F has η the do-nothing of A; for lists, η(x) is the empty list and the join reverses a list; the list wrapper is forgetting after freeing","For a left-and-right pair F ⊣ U, the wrapper is F ∘ U; for lists, η(x) = [x, x] and the join keeps the first list; lists are free because they can be undone","Only groups give wrappers: U ∘ F is 47b2’s free group; for lists, η(x) = [x] and the join deletes repeated letters; the list wrapper forgets the letters"],"why":"Each of 47f1’s left-and-right pairs gives a wrapper this way."},
 {"u":"∞1.5.1","q":"What does the chance-row technique say, and why is the result a chance list again? Why is the chance of a 6 in the example 1/3?","a":"For chance rules f : A → T(B) and g : B → T(C), (g ⋆ f)(a) gives c the chance Σ over b of f(a)(b) · g(b)(c), and as grids ⋆ is 30p1’s product of chance grids; the chances over c add to Σ over b of f(a)(b) = 1, so it is a chance list again; in the example ½ · 1/6 + ½ · ½ = 1/3","w":["(g ⋆ f)(a) gives c the chance f(a)(b) + g(b)(c); the result adds to 2 and is halved; the chance of a 6 is ½ + 1/6 halved","(g ⋆ f)(a) gives c the largest of f(a)(b) · g(b)(c) over b; it is a chance list because each term is at most 1; the chance of a 6 is ½ · ½ = ¼","(g ⋆ f)(a) gives c the chance Σ over b of f(a)(b) · g(b)(c); it adds to 1 only for fair rules; the chance of a 6 is 1/6 + ½ = 2/3"],"why":"Every route through b, its chance times the next."},
 {"u":"∞1.6.1","q":"Why can two well-made wrappers not always be joined into one? Why must the row open the wrapper before g runs?","a":"With T ∘ R an R sits between the two T layers, and joining them needs a well-made swap, which many pairs lack; and g takes a B while f(a) is a T(B), so the row must open the wrapper before g can run","w":["Two well-made wrappers always join, by running one after the other; the row opens the wrapper only to save time","They cannot be joined because η ⋆ f = f fails for every pair; g could run on f(a) as it stands","They can be joined exactly when both are the list wrapper; the row opens the wrapper because g is not well-made"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["∞Υ"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["∞Υ"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"∞Υ.1.1","q":"What does the slope-map technique say, and which two demands of 47b1 does it check? Why must the arrows send chosen point to chosen point for the slope grids to run in a row?","a":"Taking slope grids at the chosen points is a map of categories from pointed flat spaces with smooth maps to spaces of vectors with grids: D(g ∘ f) = D(g) D(f) and D(id) = I, the two demands of 47b1; the chain gives D(g ∘ f)(p) = Dg(f(p)) Df(p), so only when f(p) = q, the chosen point of g’s source, is that Dg at its chosen point times Df at its chosen point","w":["Taking slope grids is a map of categories that turns arrows round: D(g ∘ f) = D(f) D(g); the chosen points matter only so that the grids are square","Taking values at the chosen points is a map of categories: (g ∘ f)(p) = g(p) f(p) and id(p) = 1; the chosen points must match so that f can be undone","Taking slope grids keeps running in a row but not doing nothing, since D(id) = 0; the chosen points must match so that every slope is 1"],"why":"With one input and one output it is 13e4’s chain rule."},
 {"u":"∞Υ.2.1","q":"What are get and put in the lens x ↦ x², and what does put remember? Why can the record lens obey “putting back what you got changes nothing” while the slope lens cannot even state it?","a":"Get is x ↦ x², and put is (x, b′) ↦ b′ · 2x: a slope arriving at the output goes back multiplied by the slope of x² at the place you were, so put remembers where it stood; the record lens has A′ = A and B′ = B, so what you got can be put back, while in the slope lens A and A′ are different sets doing different jobs, and a value got is not a slope to put","w":["Get is x ↦ 2x and put is b′ ↦ b′ · x²; put remembers the output; the slope lens cannot state the rule because it has no get","Get is x ↦ x² and put is b′ ↦ √b′, the undoing; put remembers nothing; the slope lens obeys the rule, since put undoes get","Get is (x, b′) ↦ b′ · 2x and put is x ↦ x²; put remembers the slope; the record lens cannot state the rule, since names and ages are different sets"],"why":"What a lens keeps is the shape: forward, then back, remembering where it stood."},
 {"u":"∞Υ.3.1","q":"What does the lens-chain technique say about brackets? Why does put of a chain need get₁(a), and not a, when it calls the second lens?","a":"Lenses in a row are a lens, and running them in a row does not care about brackets: (L₃ ∘ L₂) ∘ L₁ = L₃ ∘ (L₂ ∘ L₁), with a do-nothing lens, so lenses are a category; the second lens stood at get₁(a), where the first lens’s get landed, so its put must be told that place: put(a, c′) = put₁(a, put₂(get₁(a), c′))","w":["Lenses in a row are a lens only when the brackets sit to the left; put needs get₁(a) because the first lens has no put","Running lenses in a row does not care about brackets, and neither does the order of the lenses; put could use a as well, since get₁ changes nothing","Lenses in a row are not a lens, since the puts do not meet; put needs get₁(a) to undo the first lens before the second runs"],"why":"The gets agree by 9b’s brackets; the puts are the same line."},
 {"u":"∞Υ.4.1","q":"What does the slope-lens technique say? Why does each backward step use only what its lens stored on the way forward and the row that arrives?","a":"Sending f to its slope lens is a map of categories from flat spaces with smooth maps to lenses: the slope lens of g after f is the slope lens of g run after the slope lens of f; each put is the arriving row times the slope grid at the place the lens stood, put(x, b′) = b′ · Df(x), so it needs only the stored x and the row that arrives","w":["Sending f to its slope lens turns arrows round, so the lens of g ∘ f is the lens of f after the lens of g; each backward step must run the whole forward pass again","The slope lens of g ∘ f is the sum of the two slope lenses; each backward step uses the outputs of every later layer, which is why it costs far more than the forward step","Sending f to its slope lens keeps running in a row only for one input and one output; each backward step uses only the error, and stores nothing"],"why":"That is why 28·now.3’s backward step costs about what the forward step cost."},
 {"u":"∞Υ.5.1","q":"What two slopes does a learner’s put return, and what happens to each? Why does a step of 0.6 not come with the promise that the error falls, for x = 2?","a":"One slope for the knobs, saying how to turn them, and one for the input, to send further back; the update turns the knobs against their slope and passes the input’s slope to whoever made the input; the technique promises a fall only for 0 < ε < 2/x², which is 0.5 for x = 2, and 0.6 is above it","w":["One slope for the output and one for the error; both are thrown away after the step; 0.6 fails the promise because the step must be above 2/x² = 0.5","One slope for the knobs and one for the input; both are used to turn the knobs; 0.6 fails the promise because the slope is 0 at x = 2","Two slopes for the knobs; the input gets none; 0.6 fails the promise because a step may never be above 0.1"],"why":"−εs² + ½ε²s²x² is below 0 exactly when 0 < ε < 2/x² and s ≠ 0."},
 {"u":"∞Υ.6.1","q":"What does the learner-chain technique say the first learner receives as the slope at its output? Why does the example’s −0.5 for w₁ match the slope of ½(w₂ w₁ x − t)² in w₁?","a":"The slope the second learner returns for its input: the chain’s put is the second learner’s put followed by the first’s, fed the second’s input slope; in the example the second returns −1 · w₂ = −0.5 for its input, the first returns −0.5 · x = −0.5 for w₁, and directly the slope of ½(w₂ w₁ x − t)² in w₁ is (c − t) w₂ x = −1 · 0.5 · 1 = −0.5","w":["The slope the second learner returns for its knobs, −2; the first returns −2 · x for w₁, and the direct slope is (c − t) w₁ x","The error itself, ℓ = ½; the first learner returns ℓ · x for w₁, which matches because x = 1","Nothing: the first learner is turned by the second’s knob slope; −0.5 matches the direct slope only by chance, since w₂ = 0.5"],"why":"The knob slopes it returns are the slopes of the error in each knob."},
 {"u":"∞Υ.7.1","q":"Why can the slopes of a layered machine not be multiplied in either order? Why did rung 13’s chain rule never need to say which slope comes first?","a":"The slopes are grids, and grids in a row do not turn about: Df = (1, 1; 0, 1) and Dg = (1, 0; 1, 1) give (2, 1; 1, 1) one way and (1, 1; 1, 2) the other, so the slopes must be met last layer first; with one input and one output the slopes are numbers, and numbers multiply in either order","w":["Because the layers have different numbers of knobs; rung 13 never said it because its chain rule had only one function","Because slopes add and do not multiply; rung 13 never said it because it always wrote the outer slope first","They can be multiplied in either order, as grids turn about; rung 13 said so in 13e4"],"why":"Rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["∞Φ"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["∞Φ"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"∞Φ.1.1","q":"What does the pull-data technique say, and why is J ∘ F data on S? Why is pulling back called reading through the map?","a":"For F : S → T and data J on T, Δ_F(J) = J ∘ F is data on S, and a map of data on T pulls back to a map of data on S; J ∘ F is two maps of categories in a row, which are one (47b1), from S to sets; it is reading through the map because each table s of the old schema is read as the rows J holds at F(s)","w":["For F : S → T and data I on S, Δ_F(I) = F ∘ I is data on T; it is data because F is undoable; reading through means copying every row twice","Δ_F(J) = J ∘ F is data on T again; it is data because J keeps sums; reading through means the rows of S are thrown away","Δ_F(J) is the set of maps from J to F; it is data on S only when F is one-to-one; reading through means running F backwards"],"why":"Each square for an arrow of S is a square for its image in T."},
 {"u":"∞Φ.2.1","q":"What does the glue-and-join technique say, and which side of Δ is the gluing on? Why do 3 and 2 rows give 5 glued rows and 6 joined rows?","a":"For F sending A and B to C, Σ(I)(C) = I(A) ⊔ I(B), the rows of both tables side by side, is left of Δ, and Π(I)(C) = I(A) × I(B), the pairs of rows, is right of Δ, so the gluing is on the left; side by side 3 + 2 = 5 rows, and pairs 3 · 2 = 6","w":["Σ(I)(C) = I(A) × I(B) is left of Δ and Π(I)(C) = I(A) ⊔ I(B) is right; gluing is on the right; 3 · 2 = 5 glued and 3 + 2 = 6 joined","Σ and Π are both undoings of Δ, so they agree up to a matching; 5 and 6 differ because one row is counted twice","Σ(I)(C) = I(A) ⊔ I(B) is right of Δ, a choice of taste; the joined table has 6 rows because the two tables share one row"],"why":"47f1 fixes each up to a matching, once Δ is fixed."},
 {"u":"∞Φ.3.1","q":"What does the new-table technique say, and why does Σ give each worker a team of their own? Why is Π’s set of teams a set of one?","a":"Moving a table of workers forward into the schema with teams, Σ gives each worker a team of their own and Π puts every worker on one team; a map out of Σ(I) is fixed on the workers, so Σ makes the freest choice, Team = the workers with team the do-nothing, where no two share a team unless forced; no arrow of T leaves Team, so Team asks nothing, and a product of nothing is a set of one","w":["Σ puts every worker on one team and Π gives each a team of their own; Σ is free because it joins rows; Π’s set of teams is empty, since no arrow leaves Team","Both Σ and Π leave the teams empty, since pulling back forgets them; a set of one would need a column out of Team","Σ gives each worker a team of their own because workers are a product; Π’s set of teams has one member for each worker"],"why":"A migration tool must say which it means, and these are the only two the arrows allow."},
 {"u":"∞Φ.4.1","q":"What does the query-is-map technique say, and where do the ands appear? Why does the pattern for “pairs of workers on one team” have one Team row?","a":"The answers to a query with only ands and somes in data I match the maps of data from its pattern Q into I; the ands are the squares, since naturality keeps the columns: team(α(w₁)) = α(t) = team(α(w₂)); the pattern has one Team row because both Worker rows must point to the same t, the some of the query","w":["The answers are the rows of the pattern Q; the ands are the pattern’s extra rows; one Team row because every schema has one team","The answers match the maps from the data I into the pattern Q; the ands are the tables; one Team row because Π puts every worker on one team","The answers match maps Q ⇒ I only when I has two workers; the ands are the somes; one Team row because the two workers are the same row"],"why":"The ands are the squares, the somes are the pattern’s extra rows."},
 {"u":"∞Φ.5.1","q":"Why does reading back after gluing forward not return the data? Which forward move gives each worker a team, and which gives one team for all?","a":"Left of is not undoing: for two tables into one, Δ(Σ(I)) puts the glued table at both A and B, so A comes back with B’s rows added; Σ gives each worker a team of their own, and Π puts every worker on one team","w":["Because Σ loses rows: A comes back empty; Π gives each worker a team of their own, and Σ one team for all","It does return the data, since Σ ⊣ Δ makes Δ the undoing of Σ; both Σ and Π give each worker a team of their own","Because gluing pairs the rows, 6 for 3 and 2; Δ gives each worker a team, and Σ gives one team for all"],"why":"Rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["∞r"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["∞r"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"∞r.1.1","q":"What does the sign-run technique show, and why are three blurred runs enough? Why does the blurred run of x · x − 1 end at ⊤?","a":"For x · x + 1 the blurred run gives + for every input sign, so x · x + 1 is positive for every number x; every number has one of the three signs −, 0, +, so three blurred runs cover all numbers; for x · x − 1 the run ends at 0 + (−) = − or (+) + (−), and a positive plus a negative can be anything, so it is ⊤","w":["It shows x · x + 1 is positive for three chosen numbers only; three runs are enough because the rest are assumed; x · x − 1 ends at ⊤ because − times − is unknown","It shows x · x + 1 has sign ⊤; three runs are enough because ⊤ covers everything; x · x − 1 ends at ⊤ because it is always negative","It shows x · x is + for every x; three runs are enough because 0 is not a number; x · x − 1 ends at ⊤ because the run fails"],"why":"The blur cannot decide, and rightly: the real answer depends on x."},
 {"u":"∞r.2.1","q":"What does the blur-pair technique say, and why does α(A) exist for every set A? Why is γ(α({1, 2})) larger than {1, 2}?","a":"α(A) ≤ s exactly when A ⊆ γ(s): blur is left of sharpen; α(A) is the least sign whose sharpening holds A, and such a sign exists because γ(⊤) holds every A; the least sign over {1, 2} is +, and γ(+) is all the positive numbers: blur loses information and sharpen cannot restore it","w":["γ(s) ⊆ A exactly when s ≤ α(A): sharpen is left of blur; α(A) exists because every set has a sign of its own; γ(α({1, 2})) is {1, 2} again","α(A) ≤ s exactly when A ⊆ γ(s); α(A) exists because ⊥ holds every A; γ(α({1, 2})) is larger because α sends {1, 2} to ⊤","α(A) = s exactly when A = γ(s): blur undoes sharpen; α(A) exists only for the five sets γ(s); γ(α({1, 2})) is larger because 0 is added"],"why":"47f3: between two orders, left of says exactly this."},
 {"u":"∞r.3.1","q":"What does the blur-sound technique say, and where does the proof use the blur-pair technique? Why does the proof work for any blur that is left of a sharpen?","a":"f(A) ⊆ γ(f♯(α(A))) for every set A: whatever the blurred run says covers every real run; the blur-pair technique gives A ⊆ γ(α(A)), used first for A and again for the set f(γ(α(A))); nothing about signs was used, only that blur is left of sharpen, so any such blur gives sound checks","w":["γ(f♯(α(A))) ⊆ f(A): the blurred run says no more than the real runs; the blur-pair technique is used once, on f; the proof needs the sum table of signs","f(A) = γ(f♯(α(A))): the blurred run is exact; the blur-pair technique is not used; the proof works only for the sign blur","f(A) ⊆ γ(f♯(α(A))) only when f is multiplying; the blur-pair technique is used for s = ⊤; the proof needs the blur to be undoable"],"why":"The arrows carry the plan, and the choice of blur is let go."},
 {"u":"∞r.4.1","q":"What does the widen technique say, and why can each end jump at most once? Why is the widened answer [100, ∞) sound though the true exit value is 100?","a":"Widening, which sends an end that keeps moving to ±∞, makes the loop analysis stop after finitely many rounds, and the result still covers every run; an end that has jumped to ∞ or −∞ cannot move again, so each end jumps at most once, at most two widenings; [100, ∞) holds the true exit value 100, so no run is missed: the answer is sound, only blurred","w":["Widening shrinks the interval each round until it stops; each end can jump once because the loop runs once; [100, ∞) is sound because it leaves 100 out","Widening runs the loop all hundred rounds at once; each end jumps once for every round; [100, ∞) is not sound, and a narrowing pass is needed to make it so","Widening sends both ends to ±∞ at the start; no end ever jumps; [100, ∞) is sound because the loop never exits"],"why":"In general widening trades sharpness for stopping."},
 {"u":"∞r.5.1","q":"What does the false-alarm technique say, and which technique of 41e2 forces it? Why may a blurred check say unsafe for a safe program but never safe for an unsafe one?","a":"A machine check that never says “safe” for an unsafe program must say “unsafe” for some safe one; with no false alarm it would decide which codes are safe, and safety is a non-trivial property of what machines do, which 41e2’s behaviour technique forbids; the blurred run covers every real run, so a blurred “safe” is true of every run, while the blur loses information and may fail to see that a safe program is safe","w":["A check that never says “safe” wrongly never says “unsafe” wrongly either, by 41e2’s behaviour technique; the blurred run is exact","Every machine check must wrongly say “safe” for some unsafe program, by 41d2’s no-stop-decider; the blurred check errs on that side","A check can be right both ways once the blur is fine enough; 41e2 forbids only checks that never stop"],"why":"41e2 leaves exactly one door open: wrongly unsafe, never wrongly safe."},
 {"u":"∞r.6.1","q":"Why must (+) + (−) be ⊤ and not 0? Why does a sound check still raise false alarms?","a":"A positive and a negative can add to any sign, so only ⊤ covers every case, and 0 would leave real runs out; sound means never wrongly safe, and by 41e2 a check that is never wrongly safe must say unsafe for some safe program","w":["Because signs cannot be added at all; a sound check raises false alarms only when it has a bug","Because (+) + (−) is always 0 for whole numbers; a sound check never raises a false alarm","Because ⊤ says the run fails; a sound check raises false alarms because widening is wrong"],"why":"⊤ says the blur does not know, not that a run fails."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["∞Τ"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["∞Τ"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"∞Τ.1.1","q":"What does the map-keeps-outputs technique say, and what must a map of machines keep? Why is a reading machine a map from X to 2 × X^Σ?","a":"A map of stream machines sends each state to a state with the same row of outputs; a map of machines h must keep the shape: out(h(x)) = out(x) and next(h(x)) = h(next(x)); a reading machine says of each state whether it accepts, a member of 2, and for each letter of Σ the state it moves to, a function from Σ to X","w":["A map of machines sends each state to a state with a longer row of outputs; it must keep only the first output; a reading machine reads two letters at a time from Σ","A map of stream machines is one-to-one on states; it must keep the number of states; a reading machine has 2 states for each letter of Σ","A map of machines sends outputs to states; it must keep next and may change out; a reading machine maps X to 2 × X^Σ because Σ has two letters"],"why":"By induction on n, next commutes with h."},
 {"u":"∞Τ.2.1","q":"What does the bisimulation technique say, and what must be checked to use it? Why does checking one step suffice?","a":"States related by a bisimulation give the same output at every step; to use it, write down a relation holding the two states and check that x R y gives out(x) = out(y) and next(x) R next(y); one step suffices because the relation survives it, so the next states are related again, and the induction is done once for all inside the technique","w":["States related by any relation give the same first output; nothing need be checked; one step suffices because machines repeat after one step","States with the same first output are related by a bisimulation; check only out(x) = out(y); one step suffices because the tails are never read","Two machines behave alike only when one maps into the other; check that the map is onto; one step suffices because the map is undoable"],"why":"A bisimulation is a certificate."},
 {"u":"∞Τ.3.1","q":"What does the last-machine technique say, and why is the map forced? Why do two states behave the same exactly when they have one image?","a":"Every stream machine X has exactly one map into the streams, x ↦ (out(x), out(next(x)), out(next²(x)), …); the map-keeps-outputs technique forces h(x) to have the outputs of x at every place, and that choice does keep out and next; so a state’s image is its whole row of outputs, and two states behave the same exactly when those rows, their images, are one","w":["Every stream machine has many maps into the streams, one for each start; the map is forced only for finite machines; two states behave the same when their first outputs agree","The streams have exactly one map into every stream machine; the map is forced by 35c1; two states behave the same when they are the same state","Every stream machine is matched with the streams; the map is forced because it is undoable; two states have one image only if the machine has one state"],"why":"A behaviour is pinned down by the one map of machines into the streams."},
 {"u":"∞Τ.4.1","q":"What does the relation-proof technique say, and what relation proves s = a(0)? Why does the second pair lead back to the first?","a":"Streams related by a bisimulation on the streams are equal; the relation is R = {(s, a(0)), (tail s, a(1))}: the first pair has heads 0 and tails in the second pair, and the second pair has heads 1 and tails s and a(0), since tail² s = s and tail a(1) = a(0), which is the first pair again","w":["Streams with equal heads are equal; the relation is {(s, a(0))} alone; the second pair is not needed","Streams related by a bisimulation agree at the first two places; the relation is {(s, a(1)), (tail s, a(0))}; the second pair leads on to a third pair, without end","Two streams are equal only by induction on places; the relation holds every pair of streams; the second pair leads back because all streams have head 0"],"why":"Two pairs, one step each; the endless row of places was handled once."},
 {"u":"∞Τ.5.1","q":"What does the least-machine technique say, and what are the states of the image? Why do q₀ and q₂ have one image in the example?","a":"The image of a reading machine in the last one accepts the same strings and has no more states than any machine that does, among those whose every state can be reached; the states of the image are the leftovers of L, the sets {w : uw in L} for strings u; q₀ and q₂ are both accepting and both go to q₁ on a, so they accept the same strings, those with an even number of a’s, and map to one language","w":["The image has more states than any machine for the pattern; its states are the strings of L; q₀ and q₂ have one image because both are start states","The image accepts fewer strings and so has fewer states; its states are the letters of Σ; q₀ and q₂ have one image because q₁ lies between them","The image is the machine itself; its states are the reachable states; q₀ and q₂ have different images, since q₂ is reached only after two a’s"],"why":"A map onto a set has at least as many inputs as the set has members."},
 {"u":"∞Τ.6.1","q":"What does the last-is-matched technique say, and why is h ∘ c the do-nothing? Why is there no last machine among sets for “any set of nexts”?","a":"If Z is a last machine for the shape F, its map c : Z → F(Z) is undoable; F(Z) is a machine by F(c), so there is the one map h : F(Z) → Z, and h ∘ c is a map of machines from Z to Z, where the do-nothing is the only one; for a set of nexts this would match Z with its parts, Z ≅ 𝒫(Z), which 35c1 forbids","w":["If Z is a last machine then c is one-to-one and not onto; h ∘ c is the do-nothing because c is; there is no last machine for sets of nexts because 3a18’s foundation forbids every machine","Every machine is matched with one step of itself; h ∘ c is the do-nothing by the bisimulation technique; there is no last machine because streams are endless","If Z is last, c has no undoing; h ∘ c is the do-nothing because Z has one state; a last machine for any set of nexts exists and is 𝒫(ℕ)"],"why":"For streams the matching says a stream is its head and its tail."},
 {"u":"∞Τ.7.1","q":"At which step does trying to extend the relation fail? Why is there no last machine among sets for any set of nexts?","a":"At the third place: 0, 1, 0, 1, … and 0, 1, 1, 0, … agree in their heads, 0, and their tails agree in their heads, 1, but the next tails have heads 0 and 1; and a last machine for any set of nexts would match Z with its parts, which 35c1 forbids","w":["At the first place, since the heads differ; and there is no last machine because machines with sets of nexts never stop","It never fails: equal heads make a bisimulation; and there is no last machine because foundation forbids streams","At the second place, since the tails differ at once; and there is no last machine because Z would have to be finite"],"why":"Only a relation that survives every step shows two states behave the same."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["∞x"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["∞x"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"∞x.1.1","q":"What does the balance technique say, and which two moves would break it? Why can A ⊢ A ⊗ A not be proved?","a":"In a proof with only ⊗ and ⊸ and no copying or throwing away, every letter’s signed count is 0; copying a given and dropping a given would each change the count; in A ⊢ A ⊗ A the letter A counts −1 on the left and +2 on the right, which is 1 and not 0","w":["Every letter appears an even number of times; joining with ⊗ and moving across ⊢ would break it; A ⊢ A ⊗ A fails because A appears three times","Every letter’s signed count is 1; the starting move and ⊸ would break it; A ⊢ A ⊗ A fails because its count is 0","Every given is spent at least once; only dropping would break it; A ⊢ A ⊗ A fails because ⊗ needs two different letters"],"why":"The count is +1 to the right of ⊢, −1 to the left, and flipped once more inside the left side of a ⊸."},
 {"u":"∞x.2.1","q":"What does the two-ands technique say, and why is A ⊗ B ⊢ A not provable? Why must A & B be reached by reaching A and reaching B from the same givens?","a":"A & B ⊢ A and A & B ⊢ B, but A ⊗ B ⊬ A; from both at once B cannot be left unspent, since B counts −1; and A & B gives only the one you choose, so either must be reachable, each from the same givens, though only one will be taken","w":["A ⊗ B ⊢ A and A ⊗ B ⊢ B, but A & B ⊬ A; A ⊗ B ⊢ A fails because A is copied; A & B needs the givens split in two, one share for A and one for B","A & B and A ⊗ B both give A; A ⊗ B ⊢ A is provable once B is marked; A & B is reached from A alone","A & B ⊢ A ⊗ B; A ⊗ B ⊢ A fails because A counts +2; A & B needs twice the givens, since both sides are received"],"why":"Ordinary “and” is both at once with free throwing away."},
 {"u":"∞x.3.1","q":"What does the spend-to-get technique say, and what is left after one coffee? Why can two coins and one machine not give coffee ⊗ coffee?","a":"A, A ⊸ B ⊢ B spends both givens, and coin ⊗ coin, coin ⊸ coffee ⊢ coffee ⊗ coin; one coin is left, and the machine is used up; coffee ⊗ coffee has count +2 for coffee against −1 from the one machine, so it cannot be reached","w":["A, A ⊸ B ⊢ B keeps the machine for another use, so two coins give coffee ⊗ coffee; nothing is left","A ⊸ B ⊢ B with no A spent; one coffee and both coins are left; coffee ⊗ coffee fails only because the coins are joined by ⊗","A, A ⊸ B ⊢ A ⊗ B; the coin and the coffee are both left; coffee ⊗ coffee fails because coin counts +2"],"why":"Ordinary logic, which may copy “coin ⇒ coffee”, would give two."},
 {"u":"∞x.4.1","q":"What does the bang-copy technique say, and how is A ⇒ B read in the new logic? Why does λf. λx. f (f x) need the mark on f and not on x?","a":"!A ⊢ !A ⊗ !A and !A, B ⊢ B: only marked givens may be copied or dropped; A ⇒ B is read as !A ⊸ B, given as many A as needed, one B; the term uses f twice and x once, so its type is !(A ⊸ A) ⊸ (A ⊸ A)","w":["A ⊢ !A for every A: any given may be marked; A ⇒ B is read as A ⊸ !B; the term uses x twice and f once, so the mark goes on x","!A ⊢ A ⊗ A and nothing may be dropped; A ⇒ B is read as !(A ⊸ B); the term needs the mark on both f and x","!A ⊢ !A ⊗ !A, but a marked given may not be dropped; A ⇒ B is read as A ⊸ B; the term needs no mark, since f is a function"],"why":"Ordinary logic sits inside the new one, behind the mark."},
 {"u":"∞x.5.1","q":"What does the no-straight-copy technique say, and where is straightness in each slot used? Why is the missing copy rule of the logic the missing copier of qubits?","a":"For a space V other than {0}, the function v ↦ v ⊗ v is not straight; ⊗ is straight in each slot, so (2v) ⊗ (2v) = 4 (v ⊗ v), where a straight map would give 2 (v ⊗ v); a qubit’s gates are straight maps, so no gate copies every state: the logic without copying is the logic of the tensor product","w":["For every space V, v ↦ v ⊗ v is straight; each slot gives (2v) ⊗ (2v) = 2 (v ⊗ v); qubits can be copied, and the logic lacks the rule by choice","v ↦ v ⊗ v is not straight because v ⊗ v = 0; straightness in each slot gives (2v) ⊗ (2v) = 0; gates are not straight maps, so they cannot copy","v ↦ v ⊗ v is straight only on {0} because ⊗ is not straight in either slot; the missing rule is the missing gate that throws a state away"],"why":"4 (v ⊗ v) ≠ 2 (v ⊗ v), since v ⊗ v ≠ 0."},
 {"u":"∞x.6.1","q":"What does one coin with the two machines give, and why? Why does A & B give A when A ⊗ B does not?","a":"One drink and not coffee ⊗ tea: both at once needs both coins, and with one, coin counts −1 + 1 + 1 = 1; and A & B is a choice, which may take the left side, while from A ⊗ B, both at once, B cannot be thrown away","w":["Coffee ⊗ tea, since the coin may be spent in each machine; and A & B gives A because & copies A","Nothing, since two machines need two coins before either works; and A ⊗ B gives A too","Coffee ⊗ coin, since the second machine gives the coin back; and A & B gives A because B is marked"],"why":"Rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["∞Η"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["∞Η"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"∞Η.1.1","q":"What does the detour-is-composing technique say, and which rule is running in a row? Why are “the same both ways” and “0 only for x itself” extra to the category?","a":"The detour rule and d(x, x) = 0 are exactly 47a1’s demands, running in a row and the do-nothing arrow, for a category whose arrows carry numbers and in which “at most” stands for “is”; the detour rule d(x, z) ≤ d(x, y) + d(y, z) is running in a row; the arrows keep only composing and the do-nothing, so a one-way street still gives a category, and two points may be at distance 0 without being one","w":["All four rules of a distance are 47a1’s demands; “the same both ways” is running in a row; nothing is extra, since a category needs every arrow undone","The detour rule and “never negative” are the demands of a category; d(x, x) = 0 is running in a row; the other two are extra because numbers cannot be composed","The detour rule alone makes a category; “0 only for x itself” is the do-nothing arrow; “the same both ways” is extra because arrows always run both ways"],"why":"Brackets cannot matter, since adding numbers ignores them."},
 {"u":"∞Η.2.1","q":"What does the short-row technique say? Why is reading the first coordinate short and doubling not?","a":"Short maps in a row are short, and the do-nothing map is short, so spaces with a distance and short maps form a category; reading (x₁, x₂) as x₁ is short since |x₁ − y₁| ≤ √((x₁ − y₁)² + (x₂ − y₂)²); doubling sends distance |x − y| to 2|x − y|, which is more, so it is continuous but not short","w":["Short maps in a row may stretch, so only the do-nothing map is short; reading the first coordinate is short because it is one-to-one; doubling is not short because it is not continuous","Short maps in a row are short; reading the first coordinate is short because it keeps every distance exactly; doubling is not short because it is not onto","Every continuous map is short; reading the first coordinate and doubling are both short, since each is continuous"],"why":"A short map never stretches a distance: d(f(x), f(y)) ≤ d(x, y)."},
 {"u":"∞Η.3.1","q":"What does the distances-to technique say, and which technique of 34a1 gives the bound? Why is the largest gap reached at z = y?","a":"The largest gap between d(−, x) and d(−, y) is exactly d(x, y): a point is placed, distances and all, by its distances to every point; 34a1’s back-triangle technique gives |d(z, x) − d(z, y)| ≤ d(x, y) for every z; at z = y the gap is |d(y, x) − 0| = d(x, y), and a bound that is reached is the largest","w":["The smallest gap between d(−, x) and d(−, y) is d(x, y); the detour rule gives the bound; the gap is reached at z = x, where both distances are 0","The largest gap is at most d(x, y) and is never reached; 34a1’s back-triangle technique gives the bound; at z = y the gap is 0","The sum of d(z, x) and d(z, y) is exactly d(x, y) for every z; “the same both ways” gives the bound; it is reached at z = y because d(y, y) = d(x, y)"],"why":"So every space with a distance sits inside a space of functions without any distance changing."},
 {"u":"∞Η.4.1","q":"What does the two-point technique say, and why are the two weights equal? Why is the magnitude of two points near 1 when they are close and near 2 when they are far apart?","a":"Two points at distance d have magnitude 2/(1 + e^(−d)), and three points each at distance d from the others have 3/(1 + 2e^(−d)); subtracting the two rows gives (1 − q)(w₁ − w₂) = 0 with q = e^(−d) < 1 for d > 0, so w₁ = w₂; close together e^(−d) is near 1 and the magnitude near 2/2 = 1, far apart e^(−d) is near 0 and it is near 2","w":["Two points have magnitude 2/(1 − e^(−d)); the weights are equal because Z has 1 on the diagonal; close together the magnitude is near 2 and far apart near 1","Two points have magnitude 2 at every distance; the weights are both 1; the magnitude changes only for three points","Two points have magnitude 1 + e^(−d); the weights are equal by choice of w; close together the magnitude is near 2, since both points are counted in full"],"why":"At d = 1 two points have magnitude about 1.46."},
 {"u":"∞Η.5.1","q":"What does the scale-count technique say, and why does the magnitude tend to 1 as t → 0? How does the growth of a segment’s magnitude give its dimension?","a":"For two points, and for three at equal distances, the magnitude of the space scaled by t runs from 1 as t → 0 to the number of points as t → ∞; as t → 0, e^(−td) → e⁰ = 1, so 2/(1 + e^(−td)) → 2/2 = 1, all points at one place; a segment of length L has magnitude 1 + L/2, so scaled by t it grows like t: dimension 1","w":["The magnitude runs from the number of points as t → 0 down to 1 as t → ∞; as t → 0, e^(−td) → 0; a segment’s magnitude does not grow, so its dimension is 0","The magnitude stays the number of points at every scale; as t → 0 it is undefined; a segment has magnitude L², so it grows like t²","The magnitude runs from 0 to the number of points; as t → 0 the weights vanish; a segment has magnitude 1 + L/2, which grows like t: dimension 2, one for each end"],"why":"Magnitude counts points at the scale they are seen."},
 {"u":"∞Η.6.1","q":"What does row one of Zw give for w = (1, 1) and two points at distance 1? Why is doubling not a short map?","a":"About 1.37 and not 1, since row i of Zw also holds e^(−d(i,j)) wⱼ for every j ≠ i, here 1 + e^(−1); and doubling stretches: 2|x − y| ≤ |x − y| + |x − y| is the detour rule, not shortness, which would need 2|x − y| ≤ |x − y|","w":["Exactly 1, so w = (1, 1) is the weighting; and doubling is short, by the detour rule","About 1.46, the magnitude; and doubling is not short because it is not continuous","2, one for each point; and doubling is not short because it is not the same both ways"],"why":"Rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["∞v"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["∞v"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"∞v.1.1","q":"What does the size-only technique say, and why is F[σ] undoable? Why are there (n − 1)! ways to seat n labels round a table?","a":"For a kind F, the number of structures on U depends only on the size of U; for a matching σ, F[σ⁻¹] ∘ F[σ] = F[id_U] = id, and the same the other way round, since F keeps running in a row and the do-nothing; and a seating of n labels round a table reads as an order from each of its n seats, so the n! orders give n!/n = (n − 1)! seatings","w":["The number of structures on U depends on the labels of U; F[σ] is undoable because every function is; there are (n − 1)! seatings because one label is left standing","Every kind has n! structures on n labels; F[σ] is undoable because σ is one-to-one alone; there are (n − 1)! seatings because the table has n − 1 seats","The number of structures depends only on the size of U; F[σ] is undoable because F[U] has one member; there are (n − 1)! seatings because cycles are orders with the last label dropped"],"why":"The labels are let go; the arrows that relabel carry what matters."},
 {"u":"∞v.2.1","q":"What does the product-shadow technique say, and why are the counts stored with factorials? Why does E · E have counts 2ⁿ?","a":"The shadow of F · G is F(x) G(x); choosing the first part’s k labels gives C(n, k) f_k g_(n−k), and dividing by n! turns C(n, k) into 1/(k!(n − k)!), so the sum is the coefficient of xⁿ in F(x) G(x): the factorials make products of kinds products of functions; E · E splits U into a part and its leftover, with shadow eˣ · eˣ = e^(2x), counts 2ⁿ","w":["The shadow of F · G is F(x) + G(x); the factorials are there to keep the counts small; E · E has counts 2ⁿ because each set is counted twice","The shadow of F · G is F(G(x)); the factorials count the orders of the labels; E · E has counts n², one for each pair of labels","The shadow of F · G is F(x) G(x); the factorials are there because every kind has n! structures; E · E has counts 2n, a set and its leftover for each label"],"why":"By 23j1’s read-back technique the function gives the counts back."},
 {"u":"∞v.3.1","q":"What does the shuffles-are-cycles technique say, and why is C(x) = −log(1 − x)? Why does a shuffle break into cycles?","a":"Every shuffle of U is one set of cycles, so S = E ∘ C, and the shadow gives n! shuffles; C(x) = Σ (n − 1)! xⁿ/n! = Σ xⁿ/n = −log(1 − x), the series of 23; follow each label to where the shuffle sends it until it returns: that is one cycle, and every label lies on exactly one","w":["Every shuffle is one cycle, so S = C; C(x) = Σ xⁿ = 1/(1 − x); a shuffle breaks into cycles because labels may be sent to two places","Every shuffle is an order of cycles, so S = L ∘ C; C(x) = eˣ − 1; a shuffle breaks into cycles because it fixes every label","Every set of cycles is a shuffle, but not every shuffle a set of cycles; C(x) = −log(1 − x) because cycles have n! structures; following a label may never return"],"why":"E(C(x)) = e^(−log(1 − x)) = 1/(1 − x), read back as n!."},
 {"u":"∞v.4.1","q":"What does the not-the-same-kind technique say, and which shuffle is fixed by every relabelling? Why does no relabelling other than the do-nothing fix an order?","a":"For n ≥ 2 there is no matching of L[U] with S[U] that commutes with every relabelling; the do-nothing shuffle is fixed by every relabelling, since σ id σ⁻¹ = id; a relabelling that moves a label changes who stands somewhere in the line, so it fixes no order, and a matching that commutes would send the fixed shuffle to a fixed order","w":["There is no matching of L[U] with S[U] at all, since their counts differ; the shuffle fixed by every relabelling is the one that moves every label; an order is fixed by every relabelling","Orders and shuffles are the same kind, since both have n! structures; no shuffle is fixed by a relabelling; a relabelling fixes the order it was made from","There is no matching that commutes, for n ≥ 2; the fixed shuffle is the full cycle; no relabelling fixes an order because orders have no first label"],"why":"That is 47c3’s V and V* again: matched by counting, not naturally."},
 {"u":"∞v.5.1","q":"What does the two-ways technique say, and which two kinds share a shadow in it? Why does a function from {1, …, n} to itself break into cycles with trees hanging?","a":"The doubly rooted trees on n labels, trees with an ordered pair of marked dots, are as many as the functions from {1, …, n} to itself, nⁿ, so there are nⁿ⁻² trees; L and S share the shadow 1/(1 − x), so L ∘ T and S ∘ T share 1/(1 − T(x)); follow f from each dot until it repeats: the dots that return lie on cycles, and every other dot hangs in a rooted tree leading to one of them","w":["The rooted trees on n labels are as many as the functions, nⁿ; E and C share a shadow; a function breaks into cycles because it is one-to-one","The doubly rooted trees are as many as the shuffles, n!, so there are n!/n² trees; L and T share a shadow; a function breaks into cycles because every dot returns","The trees are as many as the functions, nⁿ, with no marks needed; L and S are the same kind; a function has trees hanging because it is onto"],"why":"For n = 4: 16 trees and 256 functions."},
 {"u":"∞v.6.1","q":"Why does the set kind break the division by n!? Why are orders and shuffles not the same kind, though they share a shadow?","a":"A structure with symmetries comes from fewer than n! labellings: the set kind has 1 structure on n labels, and 1/n! is no count; and orders and shuffles share the shadow 1/(1 − x), but no matching of them commutes with every relabelling","w":["The set kind has n! structures, so the division gives 1 twice over; and orders and shuffles differ because their counts differ","The set kind has no structures; and orders and shuffles are not the same kind because shuffles have a larger shadow","The division by n! works for sets and fails for orders; and orders and shuffles are the same kind after all, matched by counting"],"why":"Rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
